1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:06,937
The brush uncovers a gateway: two posts, a crossbar, and every inch of it carved with small scenes.

2
00:00:06,937 --> 00:00:09,956
It is not part of a wall and it holds nothing up.

3
00:00:09,956 --> 00:00:13,154
It is there to be walked through and looked at.

4
00:00:15,667 --> 00:00:20,915
The empire that could build across the subcontinent is gone — and what replaces it is this:

5
00:00:20,915 --> 00:00:25,151
smaller states, and some of the best carving India had yet produced.

6
00:00:32,367 --> 00:00:36,828
Inscriptions carry most of the weight here, especially donor records.

7
00:00:36,828 --> 00:00:41,354
Coins tell us who ruled where, which matters when the texts are vague.

8
00:00:41,354 --> 00:00:46,625
And texts — the Puranas for dynastic lists, which are useful but not precise.

9
00:00:49,233 --> 00:00:50,066
In the north,

10
00:00:50,066 --> 00:00:54,347
Pushyamitra Shunga takes power about 185 BCE and the Shungas hold a muc

11
00:00:54,347 --> 00:00:58,927
h smaller territory from Pataliputra and Vidisha for roughly a century.

12
00:01:02,867 --> 00:01:07,261
Then the Kanvas, briefly, and the north breaks into still smaller pieces.

13
00:01:07,261 --> 00:01:14,569
The pattern to carry away is simple: after the Mauryas, no single power holds the north again for five hundred years.

14
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:19,934
The interesting story moves south.

15
00:01:19,934 --> 00:01:26,674
In the Deccan the Satavahanas build a long-lasting state, with Pratishthana — modern Paithan — among their centres.

16
00:01:26,674 --> 00:01:32,669
Their best-known ruler is Gautamiputra Satakarni, who pushed back the Shakas from the north-west.

17
00:01:38,467 --> 00:01:42,408
We know about him partly from an inscription at Nashik set up by his mother,

18
00:01:42,408 --> 00:01:46,751
Gautami Balashri, which is one of the more remarkable documents of the period.

19
00:01:50,267 --> 00:01:54,187
Two things about the Satavahanas are worth knowing for the exam.

20
00:01:54,187 --> 00:01:59,976
First, matronymics: their rulers took their mothers' names — Gautamiputra means son of Gautami,

21
00:01:59,976 --> 00:02:04,558
Vasishthiputra son of Vasishthi — which is unusual and much discussed.

22
00:02:09,933 --> 00:02:12,040
Second, their coins and their patronage.

23
00:02:12,040 --> 00:02:19,278
They issued lead and other coins, and they and the merchants of their ports funded rock-cut monasteries along the Deccan trade routes.

24
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:29,178
And then the art, which is why this period matters beyond dynastic lists.

25
00:02:29,178 --> 00:02:33,745
At Sanchi and Bharhut in the centre, and later at Amaravati in the south-east,

26
00:02:33,745 --> 00:02:38,150
stupas were enlarged and surrounded with carved railings and gateways.

27
00:02:42,633 --> 00:02:46,585
Look closely at the panels and you will see crowds, animals, trees,

28
00:02:46,585 --> 00:02:52,222
cities — and at the centre of each story an empty seat, a tree, a pair of footprints or a wheel.

29
00:02:52,222 --> 00:02:55,069
The Buddha is in every scene and is never shown.

30
00:02:55,069 --> 00:02:58,914
That is a deliberate convention, and it holds for centuries.

31
00:03:03,900 --> 00:03:05,887
Their dates are genuinely unsettled.

32
00:03:05,887 --> 00:03:09,323
The Puranic lists, the inscriptions and the coins do not agree,

33
00:03:09,323 --> 00:03:13,028
so one group of historians places the dynasty's beginning soon after

34
00:03:13,028 --> 00:03:17,032
the Mauryas and another puts its real power a century or more later.

35
00:03:22,633 --> 00:03:25,169
Textbooks usually give the earlier range.

36
00:03:25,169 --> 00:03:31,749
Know the rulers and the achievements; hold the dates loosely, and say so if a question presses on them.

37
00:03:35,467 --> 00:03:38,667
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

38
00:03:38,667 --> 00:03:39,760
North splits.

39
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:41,152
Deccan rises.

40
00:03:46,433 --> 00:03:47,260
Dig Quiz.

41
00:03:47,260 --> 00:03:48,830
Pause and name it.

42
00:03:48,830 --> 00:03:54,118
First: the carved gateway of a stupa, of which Sanchi has four.

43
00:03:54,118 --> 00:03:55,244
A torana.

44
00:03:58,633 --> 00:04:03,373
Second: the naming practice by which a Satavahana ruler took his mother's name.

45
00:04:03,373 --> 00:04:04,202
A matronymic.

46
00:04:04,202 --> 00:04:11,967
Third: the convention by which early Buddhist art shows the Buddha through a tree, a seat or footprints rather than a figure.

47
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:17,238
Aniconic representation.

48
00:04:17,238 --> 00:04:22,386
In Mains, a frequent question asks who paid for early Buddhist monuments.

49
00:04:26,067 --> 00:04:33,988
The donor inscriptions answer it: merchants, guilds, monks and nuns, and ordinary householders, far more often than kings.

50
00:04:37,533 --> 00:04:43,200
A gateway takes its place in the gallery, carved by people whose names are cut into its railings.

51
00:04:45,967 --> 00:04:52,072
Next gallery: rulers arriving from the north-west, and the coins that are often all we know them by.

52
00:04:52,072 --> 00:04:55,818
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

